    <h3>Military Operations and Zulu Time</h3>
    <p>
      Military forces coordinate global operations using UTC, which they refer to as **Zulu Time**. In the phonetic alphabet, the letter 'Z' stands for 'Zulu,' representing the zero meridian time zone. By standardizing military logs, flight manifests, and combat operations to Zulu Time, commanders ensure that forces operating across multiple continents are synchronized to the exact same clock.
    </p>

    <h3>Aviation and Weather Forecasting</h3>
    <p>
      The aviation industry depends on UTC to coordinate flight paths, air traffic control, and international departures. Pilots and air traffic managers use UTC to prevent scheduling errors during long flights. Similarly, meteorologists use UTC to coordinate weather observations. Synoptic weather maps and radar sweeps are timestamped to UTC, allowing scientists to monitor storm movements across continents concurrently.
    </p>

    <h3>Computers, Databases, and the Internet</h3>
    <p>
      In computer science, storing time in local time zones can lead to database errors, especially during daylight saving transitions. To prevent this, standard database systems store transactional records and system logs in UTC. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The **UNIX Epoch** — the system used by computers to count time — measures the number of seconds that have elapsed since January 1, 1970, at 00:00:00 UTC. Internet protocols, security certificates, and ISO 8601 timestamps are governed by UTC to maintain consistent order across servers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 9: PRACTICAL EXAMPLES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="practical-examples" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Real-World Examples">
    <h2>9. Real-World Examples &amp; Common Scenarios</h2>
    <p>
      To clarify how these standards operate, let us look at common scenarios where timezone confusion occurs:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Scheduling a Transatlantic Meeting</strong>: If you schedule a meeting for 3:00 PM GMT in November, colleagues in London will join at 3:00 PM local time. However, if you schedule the meeting for 3:00 PM GMT in June, London will be observing BST (British Summer Time, UTC+1), meaning they will join at 4:00 PM local time. If you use UTC, the baseline remains constant, preventing confusion.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tracking Flight Times</strong>: International flight boarding passes print departure and arrival times in the local time of each city. Flight dispatchers, however, schedule the flight path using UTC to calculate the exact travel duration and fuel burn without regional clock adjustments.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Analyzing Database Logs</strong>: If a software server log records a user action at 1:30 AM EST, this could represent two different moments in time during the autumn fall back transition, when 1:30 AM occurs twice. Storing timestamps in UTC prevents this confusion because the atomic baseline continues without interruption.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 10: WHEN TO USE GMT OR UTC?
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="when-to-use" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="When to Use GMT or UTC">
    <h2>10. When Should You Use GMT or UTC?</h2>
    <p>
      Determining whether to use GMT or UTC depends on the context of your communication. Review these guidelines to select the correct standard:
    </p>
    <div class="method-grid">
      <article class="method-card">
        <h3>Use GMT When...</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Speaking with people living in the UK, Ireland, or West Africa.</li>
          <li>Describing civil clocks and winter hours in London.</li>
          <li>Writing calendar events for local audiences in Greenwich timezone regions.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="method-card">
        <h3>Use UTC When...</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Programming software, setting database schemas, and writing code.</li>
          <li>Coordinating international operations across multiple time zones.</li>
          <li>Scheduling global webinars and remote team shifts.</li>
          <li>Configuring cloud servers and system logging protocols.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 11: COMMON MYTHS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="myths" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Common Myths">
    <h2>11. Common Timezone Myths Debunked</h2>
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Myth 1: "GMT and UTC have different times."</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> For civil timekeeping, their clocks show the exact same time. 12:00 PM GMT is 12:00 PM UTC. Their difference lies entirely in the underlying standard and how their time is calculated, not in the clock face.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Myth 2: "London is always in GMT."</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> London only observes GMT during the winter. In summer, the UK transitions to British Summer Time (BST, UTC+1). This means London's local clock runs one hour ahead of GMT for half the year.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Myth 3: "UTC is a timezone."</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> UTC is a global time standard, not a timezone. Time zones around the world are calculated as positive or negative offsets relative to UTC.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

